OpenSea Whitelist Checker v0.4.0
Installation and Usage Instructions
===================================

1. DOWNLOAD V0.4.0

1. Open:
   https://github.com/RohitValeecha89/opensea-whitelist-checker-releases/releases/tag/v0.4.0
2. Scroll to Assets.
3. Click OpenSea-Whitelist-Checker-Setup-0.4.0.exe.
4. Wait for the approximately 100 MB download to finish.

Do not download latest.yml or the .blockmap file. Those files are used
internally by automatic updates.


2. INSTALL THE APPLICATION

1. Double-click the downloaded EXE file.
2. If Windows SmartScreen appears:
   a. Click More info.
   b. Confirm the filename is:
      OpenSea-Whitelist-Checker-Setup-0.4.0.exe
   c. Click Run anyway.
3. Complete the installer.
4. Open OpenSea Whitelist Checker.

The SmartScreen warning may appear because the installer is not yet code-signed.


3. OBTAIN AN OPENSEA API KEY

1. Sign in to your OpenSea account.
2. Open OpenSea's developer/API section.
3. Create or request an API key.
4. Copy the key and keep it private.

Every person using the checker should use their own OpenSea API key. Do not
share the API key publicly.


4. ADD THE OPENSEA API KEY

1. Open Settings in the application.
2. Find OpenSea API key.
3. Paste your API key.
4. Click Save settings.
5. Confirm that the API key shows as saved.

Wallet checks always run one at a time. This limit is fixed to reduce OpenSea rate-limit failures.


5. ADD ETHERSCAN API KEYS

Ethereum PnL uses Etherscan V2 transaction and ERC-721 history. Create one or
more Etherscan API keys, then open Settings and paste one key per line under
Etherscan API keys. Click Save settings. Keys are encrypted locally and the app
automatically rotates/fails over between them when Etherscan limits a request.
Leave the field blank later to keep the saved keys.


6. ADD RPC URLS

RPCs are optional for eligibility checks, but an RPC for the collection chain
is required for complete ERC-721 mint counts in PnL cards.

Enter one RPC per line in Settings using:

chain=https://your-rpc-url

Example:

ethereum=https://your-ethereum-rpc
base=https://your-base-rpc
polygon=https://your-polygon-rpc
arbitrum=https://your-arbitrum-rpc
optimism=https://your-optimism-rpc

Important:
- Do not include spaces before the chain name.
- Every line must contain an equals sign.
- Use the chain slug expected by OpenSea.
- Keep RPC provider API keys private.

Click Save settings after adding the RPC URLs.


7. IMPORT WALLETS

SECURITY WARNING:
Only import dedicated, low-value mint wallets. Do not import treasury wallets,
hardware-wallet seed wallets, or wallets containing valuable assets.

1. Open Wallets.
2. Under Import private keys, enter a Group name such as Mint Wallets.
3. Paste one private key per line.
4. Each key must contain 0x followed by 64 hexadecimal characters.
5. Click Encrypt & import.
6. Review Imported, Duplicates, and Invalid lines.

After import, the app displays wallet addresses, not private keys. Keys are
encrypted locally using Windows encryption and cannot be displayed again from
the app.


8. ADD OR DISCOVER AN OPENSEA DROP

Automatic discovery:

1. Open Drops.
2. Click Discover.
3. Wait for OpenSea drops to load.
4. Find the collection you want.
5. Click Check ->.

Manual entry:

1. Open the collection on OpenSea.
2. Copy its collection URL, for example:
   https://opensea.io/collection/example-collection/overview
3. Return to the app and open Drops.
4. Paste the URL into Paste an OpenSea collection link or slug.
5. Click Add drop.
6. When the collection appears, click Check ->.

You may also enter only the collection slug, such as:

example-collection

Only genuine opensea.io collection links are accepted.


9. CHECK WHITELIST ELIGIBILITY

1. Open Checker.
2. Paste an OpenSea collection link, enter its slug, or select a saved drop.
3. Under Wallets, select All wallets or a specific wallet group.
4. Click Check.
5. The selected wallets appear immediately with Waiting status.
6. Watch each row update as that wallet is checked.

The application authenticates each wallet with OpenSea using a locally signed
login message. It requests only the read:eligibility scope and does not submit
a transaction or mint anything. The read-only token is revoked automatically after every wallet check, including
failed eligibility requests. This prevents OpenSea's 25 scoped-token limit from
accumulating across checks.


10. UNDERSTAND THE RESULTS

Each row represents a wallet. Each phase has its own column. Phases are shown in chronological mint order, and wallet allowances are matched to OpenSea's phase UUID rather than response position.

- A green check mark with 1 means the wallet is eligible, usually with one
  remaining mint.
- A green check mark with 2 means it may mint two, depending on OpenSea's
  returned allowance.
- A dash means the wallet is not eligible for that phase or has no remaining
  allowance.
- Error means that wallet could not be checked.

Hover over Error to view the message. If some rows fail, click Retry failed wallets to retry only those rows. Common causes include:
- Invalid or expired OpenSea API key
- OpenSea rate limiting
- Drop not found
- Eligibility endpoint unavailable
- Collection not using an OpenSea-supported drop configuration


11. AUTOMATIC UPDATES

The app checks for updates shortly after opening.

When an update is available:

1. A Version X is available banner appears.
2. Click Download update.
3. Wait for the download to finish.
4. Click Restart & install.

You can also open Settings and click Check for updates. GitHub login and tokens
are not required.


12. COLLECTION PNL

1. Open PnL.
2. Enter an OpenSea collection URL, collection slug, or contract address.
3. Leave Chain on Auto-detect, or select the chain if address detection fails.
4. Choose All wallets, Whole group, or Select wallets.
5. Optionally enter a card username and upload a PNG/JPEG/WebP PFP under 5 MB.
6. Click Generate PnL card.
7. Use Copy image or Download PNG.

For Ethereum collections, PnL uses paginated Etherscan V2 ERC-721 and normal
transaction history, counts zero-address transfers as mints, and calculates NFT
basis using the average known acquisition cost. Multiple configured Etherscan
keys are rotated automatically. Gas remains excluded. For other EVM chains,
the app clearly labels the result as an OpenSea estimate and can use the
configured chain RPC to verify mint counts.

Private keys are not decrypted or used for PnL. Generation continues when
switching tabs, and the latest card remains visible on return.


13. MINTING NOW

1. Open Minting Now.
2. Review collections ranked by OpenSea mint events in the last 15 minutes.
3. Each card shows image, price, recent mints, chain, and supply when available.
4. MINT ON OPENSEA opens the official OpenSea page; the app never mints itself.
5. CHECK WALLETS opens Checker with that collection selected.

The list refreshes every 60 seconds. A dash is shown when OpenSea does not
provide supply data; the app does not invent missing values.
